-- chem/h2plus-net.hypercubical — two nuclei and one electron on the smallest cage of the diamond net, with the flux's
-- coset at its lowest level. On a closed piece Gauss's law makes the total charge zero, so the cage keeps its
-- sixteen lines to the rest of the net, gathered on a wall that takes the flux out (`cage-walled`,
-- chem/netpiece.hypercubical). With G(a, u) the potential at u for unit current in at a and out at the wall, charges q
-- have energy L Σ q_a q_b G(a, b), L = λ/2. For nuclei at a and b and the electron at v:
--   L (G(a,a) + G(b,b) + 2 G(a,b))  +  L U(v),   U(v) = G(v,v) − 2 G(a,v) − 2 G(b,v),
-- and the electron hops along the cage's lines with amplitude t. The potentials are given, in units of 1/280, and
-- checked against Kirchhoff's law at every vertex but the wall (`hp-potentials`); the law has one solution with
-- the wall at zero. Then: for the nuclei on two three-line vertices two lines apart (0 and 1) the nuclei's part is
-- 192/280 = 24/35 and U is −13/35 on a nucleus, −1/20 between them, 13/140, 1/5, 33/140 further off (`hp-near`);
-- for the nuclei on opposite two-line vertices four lines apart (4 and 9) the nuclei's part is 3/5 and U is −43/140
-- on a nucleus, 4/35 and 27/140 elsewhere (`hp-far`). With the electron on a nucleus that vertex carries no charge
-- and the energy is the other nucleus's own, G(b,b): at t = 0 two-line vertices at any separation have the same
-- energy, 82/280. The bond (`hp-bond`): to second order in t the state spread evenly over the two nuclei gains
-- (280 t²/L) Σ c² / (2 Δ) over the vertices next to a nucleus, c the nuclei it is next to and Δ = 280 (U there − U
-- on a nucleus). For the nuclei at 4 and 5, which share the neighbour 0: Δ = 88 with c = 2 and Δ = 128 twice with
-- c = 1, the sum 1/44 + 1/128 = 43/1408. For the nuclei at 4 and 9, opposite: Δ = 118 four times with c = 1, the sum
-- 1/59. 43/1408 > 1/59: the nuclei sharing a neighbour lie lower, by (280 t²/L)(43/1408 − 1/59), with the nuclei's
-- parts 178 and 168 already counted in the equal energies at t = 0. A leaf: no module imports it.
import "netpiece.hypercubical"

